1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:01,508
When your app breaks,

2
00:00:01,568 --> 00:00:03,035
your logs should tell the story.

3
00:00:03,747 --> 00:00:04,784
Meet MXLogger.

4
00:00:04,844 --> 00:00:08,511
Cross-platform logging for iOS, Android, and Flutter.

5
00:00:09,213 --> 00:00:12,530
A native C++ core uses memory-mapped files

6
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:14,897
and FlatBuffers to write compact logs.

7
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:17,537
Encrypt log files with AES.

8
00:00:17,597 --> 00:00:20,994
And control rotation, retention, and disk usage.

9
00:00:21,713 --> 00:00:24,206
Import .mx files into the analyzer.

10
00:00:24,266 --> 00:00:28,089
Search by keyword, level, time, name, or tag.

11
00:00:28,813 --> 00:00:30,551
Expand structured JSON.

12
00:00:30,611 --> 00:00:33,556
Inspect nearby records to understand what happened.

13
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:35,821
Analyze on your desktop.

14
00:00:35,881 --> 00:00:38,369
Or inspect logs right inside your Flutter app.

15
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,911
Add MXLogger to your project.

16
00:00:40,971 --> 00:00:43,301
And start capturing useful context.

17
00:00:44,013 --> 00:00:46,912
MXLogger. Write fast. Debug smarter.

18
00:00:46,972 --> 00:00:48,803
Explore the project on GitHub.
